- async/generator codegen (legacy BBV; the baseline tier has it)
- remaining unsupported opcodes in legacy BBV: `Eval`, `BigInt`, ES module
  import ops, `using` (the baseline tier compiles all of these)
- module scripts are never compiled by any tier: the shell loads a root
  module through the module loader, which never calls the `scriptCompiled`
  extension hook, so neither the in-process batch nor snapshot
  registration sees module scripts (pre-existing, not new with baseline).
  Register module roots in both flows; needed for full AOT under MIR too.
- move abstract-interpretation transfer func for ops to types analysis
  (currently in emitter for historical reasons)
- use a DSL to write lowering cases and type transfer functions for each op?
  - maybe ISLE?
- `module_is_bigint_free` is weird and we should do better
- AOT output is not reproducible: two compiles of one input differ in the
  data segment (the wizened heap), not in code (checked 2026-10-01 on
  richards and crypto: code sections byte-identical, MIR dumps identical).
  Most of it was the wizening run executing Octane's top-level `main()`
  (fixed time, so iteration counts and heap contents vary); the nm-bench
  skill now wizens without that call. What remains is 608 bytes (richards):
  the top of the shadow stack (63 MiB) and early heap allocations
  (65-68 MiB), most likely values the runtime seeds at startup from WASI
  `random_get` and the clocks (hash-code scramblers, Math.random state,
  startup timestamps). Fix: wizen with a seeded random source and a fixed
  clock. wizer 9 builds its WasiCtx internally with no hook for either (and
  its custom-linker option excludes WASI), so this needs a wizer option
  (upstream) or a WASI shim in front of the shell.
